Package: noqtis
Type: Package
Title: Notary of Quantum-Safe Traceable Inputs and Seals
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R: c(
    person(c("Vansh", "Singh"), "Ruhela", email = "vsruhela@proton.me",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0009-0004-1750-3592", GitHub = "rootcoder007")))
Description: Post-quantum signatures, key encapsulation and sealed records,
    built on the dependency-free 'NOQTIS' C library. Generate key pairs,
    then sign and verify with the Module-Lattice-Based Digital Signature
    Algorithm (ML-DSA) of the National Institute of Standards and Technology
    (2024) <doi:10.6028/NIST.FIPS.204>, its Stateless Hash-Based Digital
    Signature Algorithm (SLH-DSA; 2024) <doi:10.6028/NIST.FIPS.205>, the
    Ed25519 signatures of Bernstein, Duif, Lange, Schwabe and Yang (2012)
    <doi:10.1007/s13389-012-0027-1>, or hybrid signatures of ML-DSA with
    Ed25519 that need both halves to verify; establish shared secrets with
    the Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM; National
    Institute of Standards and Technology, 2024) <doi:10.6028/NIST.FIPS.203>
    or with Hamming Quasi-Cyclic (HQC) key encapsulation, and seal a message
    or a file to an ML-KEM key; hash with the Secure Hash Algorithms SHA-2
    and SHA-3, the extendable-output functions SHAKE, or BLAKE2b; authenticate
    with a keyed-hash message authentication code (HMAC), and keep secret keys
    under a passphrase with Argon2id. A whole directory can be sealed under
    one signature and later verified offline; a Merkle proof shows chosen
    files to someone without revealing the others, and signed statements
    rotate or revoke a sealing key. Tables can be pinned to a schema and
    to summaries that show later drift, and results wrapped in Dead Simple
    Signing Envelopes (DSSE). Messages can be signed and encrypted in the
    Cryptographic Message Syntax (CMS) and as Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail
    Extensions (S/MIME) mail with X.509 certificates, or as 'OpenPGP' version 6
    messages; a key store keeps and certifies other people's public keys, and
    keys held on a 'PKCS #11' token sign and decapsulate without leaving it.
    Keys and nonces come from the operating system's random generator.
